93 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186265806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152293 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152293 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152293 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152293 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152293 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152293 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding General Solutions to Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs With Variable Coefficients" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152293 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152293 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "9ef8a6ef-3e77-5e3e-ac75-c3dece0af96e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152294 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152294 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152294 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152294 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152294 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152294 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152294 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186379528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152294 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186379548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152294 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152294 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152294 #edb/ref 1039 "Constructing General Solutions of Inhomogeneous ODEs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152294 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152294 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "7640be27-8277-5a9c-bf14-83bcc7a38604" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152359 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152359 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152359 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152359 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152359 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152359 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152359 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152359 #edb/ref 1045 "General Solutions of Linear ODEs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152359 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186153290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152359 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152359 #edb/ref 1050 1140.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152359 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "d59d69ae-798b-5a20-9d5f-60ce55a98799" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152577 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152577 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152577 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152577 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152577 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152577 #edb/ref 1069 "q-339880" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152577 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152577 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}-15y=-7{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above, for which the corresponding complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{5x}+B{e}^{-3x},$ where $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants. Select the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{4x}$ \n{{field-1}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{4x}$ \n{{field-2}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe general solution of the differential equation is given by\n\n$\\,\\,$\n$y(x)=$ \n{{field-3}}\n$+$\n{{field-4}},\n\n$\\,$\nwhere \n{{field-5}} are arbitrary constants." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152577 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "181a7898-7274-5974-9634-f1677406f23f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152577 #edb/ref 1074 "For an inhomogeneous differential equation, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}_{c}(x)$ is the complementary solution (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- ${y}_{p}(x)$ is a particular solution of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check which of the two given functions is a particular solution of our inhomogeneous equation:\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{4x},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =({e}^{4x})^{′}=4{e}^{4x}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(4{e}^{4x})^{′}=16{e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n(16{e}^{4x})-2(4{e}^{4x})-15({e}^{4x})=-7{e}^{4x}.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{4x}$ $\\text{is}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{4x},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =(x{e}^{4x})^{′}={e}^{4x}+4x{e}^{4x}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =({e}^{4x}+4x{e}^{4x})^{′}=8{e}^{4x}+16x{e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n(8{e}^{4x}+16x{e}^{4x})-2({e}^{4x}+4x{e}^{4x})-15(x{e}^{4x})=-7{e}^{4x}.\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{4x}$ $\\text{isn't}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nWe are told that the complementary solution of our equation is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{5x}+B{e}^{-3x}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{5x}+B{e}^{-3x}+{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A\\text{ and }B$ are arbitrary constants." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153290 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153290 #edb/ref 1061 601711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153290 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c268f62a-7fd0-540c-9ff9-832b5389a31e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153290 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153291 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153291 #edb/ref 1061 601712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153291 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f0a0cdaa-70e3-5041-a39e-64dbcbe7a4a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153291 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153292 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153292 #edb/ref 1061 601715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153292 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "66cf83ce-b3bc-50bd-88ae-2b9f54096657" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153292 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153293 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153293 #edb/ref 1061 601718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153293 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "38e288bc-7c3f-536d-a984-63e199038d6a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153293 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153294 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153294 #edb/ref 1061 601721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153294 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4499c456-0b30-59ea-92cd-0a6e8cc58d01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153294 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153295 #edb/ref 1061 601722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153295 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2779fb81-53c1-5556-969d-409a83b9a273" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153295 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153335 #edb/ref 1064 "Similar to first-order linear ODEs, for inhomogeneous differential equations, the **general solution** is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y_c(x)$ is the **complementary solution** (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- $y_p(x)$ is a **particular solution** of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWe will apply this structure to a specific differential equation in the next slide." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153335 #edb/ref 1065 14780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153335 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d323d4d9-40c7-5a00-aafe-a0c1ba46fd4c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153335 #edb/ref 1067 "General Solutions of Inhomogeneous ODEs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153382 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the second-order linear homogeneous differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' - 5y' + 6y = 0.\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that a fundamental set of solutions is given by\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = e^{3x}, \\qquad y_2(x) = e^{2x}.\n$$\n\nThe superposition principle tells us that any linear combination of solutions to a linear, homogeneous differential equation is also a solution to the equation. Therefore, the **general solution** to this differential equation is\n\n$$\ny(x) = Ae^{3x} + Be^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are **arbitrary constants.**\n\nIn general, if an $n$th order homogeneous, linear ODE has a fundamental set of solutions given by \n\n$$\ny_1(x), \\quad y_2(x),\\quad \\ldots, \\quad y_n(x),\n$$\n\nthen the general solution to the equation is \n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{y(x) = C_1y_1(x)+C_2y_2(x)+\\cdots+C_ny_n(x)}\n$$\n\nfor arbitrary constants $C_1, C_2,\\ldots C_n.$\n\n**Note:** Using sigma notation, we can write the general solution more compactly as\n\n$$\ny(x) = \\sum_{i=1}^n C_i y_i(x).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153382 #edb/ref 1065 4197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153382 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d0ae06ba-917f-56b1-8d5d-fac54f464ee8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153382 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263135 #edb/ref 1080 "y=At+Bt\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263135 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263135 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0f5c2990-0c8a-584d-bfde-31963f7ff692" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264044 #edb/ref 1080 "y=At+B{t}^{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264044 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264044 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "784e1679-3718-5a06-a425-4c1bcafd5944" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264349 #edb/ref 1080 "y=At+\\frac{B}{{t}^{2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264349 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264349 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9d9700b6-666a-5f29-a8bb-ff7cd542805c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265126 #edb/ref 1080 "y=At+\\frac{B}{t}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265126 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265126 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f1e28c2f-fe2a-57e6-bebd-5dd0362f05e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265250 #edb/ref 1080 "y=At+B{e}^{t}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265250 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265250 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ffcf23b6-c4a2-5b74-ab79-b90a02e66db9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265565 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265565 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265565 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265565 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186265126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265565 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186265250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265565 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265565 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265565 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186264349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265565 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b667bc8d-3635-50e2-acc5-7b154ab5806c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265800 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265800 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130028" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265800 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265800 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)=t$ is a solution to the equation ${t}^{2}{y}^{″}+2t{y}^{′}-2y=0$ for $t>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265800 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fa1dae64-83b4-59f1-8ce4-3c9cac8a6ed9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265800 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t)t.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}t+u \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}t+2{u}^{′}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}({u}^{″}t+2{u}^{′})+2t({u}^{′}t+u)-2ut & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{t}^{3}+2{u}^{′}{t}^{2}+2{u}^{′}{t}^{2}+2ut-2ut & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{t}^{3}+4{u}^{′}{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}({u}^{″}t+4{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}t+4{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}t+4w=0.\n$$\n\nThe solution is\n\n$$\nw=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{t}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{t}^{4}}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3{t}^{3}}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=ut$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3{t}^{3}}+{c}_{2})t.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-3$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}\\cdot t=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=At+\\frac{B}{{t}^{2}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312234 #edb/ref 1080 "isn't" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312234 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312234 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2219bbfa-a73f-5279-82c4-8e4bb0824640" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312298 #edb/ref 1080 "C{e}^{-x}" #edb/ref 17592