068 #edb/ref 17592186314340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314878 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314878 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314878 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314878 #edb/ref 1069 "q-339876" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314878 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314878 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+4y=3{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above, for which the corresponding complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{-4x},$ where $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants. Select the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{-x}$ \n{{field-1}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{-x}$ \n{{field-2}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe general solution of the differential equation is given by\n\n$\\,\\,$\n$y(x)=$ \n{{field-3}}\n$+$\n{{field-4}},\n\n$\\,$\nwhere \n{{field-5}} are arbitrary constants." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314878 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "81480660-8b23-5d75-8411-ad962650246b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314878 #edb/ref 1074 "For an inhomogeneous differential equation, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}_{c}(x)$ is the complementary solution (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- ${y}_{p}(x)$ is a particular solution of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check which of the two given functions is a particular solution of our inhomogeneous equation:\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{-x},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =({e}^{-x})^{′}=-{e}^{-x}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(-{e}^{-x})^{′}={e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n({e}^{-x})+5(-{e}^{-x})+4({e}^{-x})=3{e}^{-x}.\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{-x}$ $\\text{isn't}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{-x},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =(x{e}^{-x})^{′}={e}^{-x}-x{e}^{-x}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =({e}^{-x}-x{e}^{-x})^{′}=-2{e}^{-x}+x{e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n(-2{e}^{-x}+x{e}^{-x})+5({e}^{-x}-x{e}^{-x})+4(x{e}^{-x})=3{e}^{-x}.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{-x}$ $\\text{is}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nWe are told that the complementary solution of our equation is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{-4x}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{-4x}+x{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A\\text{ and }B$ are arbitrary constants." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355103 #edb/ref 1080 "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sec}⁡3x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355103 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355103 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "320f4b21-2707-5091-a2f6-3f46b3db3db9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355678 #edb/ref 1080 "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355678 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355678 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "732019b1-4135-57f2-b0d0-0be5c43e75f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355722 #edb/ref 1080 "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355722 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355722 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7905340a-4ac8-558d-bb3d-307b21001f47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356157 #edb/ref 1080 "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356157 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356157 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a886961d-560b-5f03-a7b4-90a1a7508fea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356637 #edb/ref 1080 "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Bx\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356637 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356637 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e1d7e1eb-54ff-5d68-969e-6e99529e477b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356985 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186355103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356985 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186355678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356985 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186355722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356985 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186356157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356985 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186356637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356985 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356985 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356985 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186356157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356985 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "296c90ca-5225-52de-bd8b-f92f0038d08f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357490 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357490 #edb/ref 1069 "q-90911" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357490 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357490 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+9y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357490 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8c4c99c3-e164-55a4-8452-aa0c671a6241" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357490 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)+9u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″} & =6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=6w\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{sec}^{2}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{sec}^{2}⁡3x\\,⟹\\,u=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x$ becomes \n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x(\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=3$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x(\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=3\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421786 #edb/ref 1080 "I, II, and III" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421786 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421786 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0246f7ce-f1b2-5e93-8e63-e7393dc569b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421787 #edb/ref 1080 "y=A{x}^{-2}+B{x}^{-1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421787 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421787 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "05fa078e-be12-5c3b-bea0-316dbe5fdadb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421788 #edb/ref 1080 "y=A{x}^{-2}+B{x}^{-2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421788 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421788 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "07ce657b-e482-5f99-bc7d-7195f7a26f4